  "src": "grid with consecutive runs of identical row vectors and consecutive runs of identical column vectors",
  "transform": "keep the first row of each identical-row run and the first column of each identical-column run",
  "grid_shape": "number of row runs x number of column runs"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  task: collapse runs of identical adjacent rows and columns to recover compressed seed pattern
  input:
    grid: 10-12 rows x 9-13 cols
    structure: |
      Horizontal bars (h-bars) and vertical bars (v-bars) on bg=0.
      Each bar is 1px wide, spanning full grid width (h) or height (v).
      Bars have distinct non-zero colors; 3-7 total (2-3 h-bars + 1-4 v-bars).
      At intersections, one bar's color is visible per a total depth ordering.
      The grid is then run-length expanded: each separator gap between bars
      is stretched to 1-6px, while bar lines stay at 1px.
    separator_runs: each >= 1, but at least one > 1 on each axis (required by solver)
    colors: 3-7 distinct non-zero colors from palette 1-8, all used for bars; bg=0
  output:
    grid: (2*h_bars+1) x (2*v_bars+1), always odd x odd, range 5-7 x 3-9
    structure: |
      Even rows are all identical: [0, vc1, 0, vc2, ...].
      Even cols are all identical: [0, hc1, 0, hc2, ...].
      Odd rows/cols correspond to bars. At (odd_r, odd_c) intersections,
      the higher-depth bar's color appears.
  transform: deduplicate consecutive identical rows, then deduplicate consecutive identical columns

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    - Pick n_h in [2,3] and n_v in [1,4] with n_h + n_v in [3,7]
    - Choose n_h + n_v distinct non-zero colors from 1-8
    - Assign a total depth ordering across all bars (a permutation)
    - Build the (2*n_h+1) x (2*n_v+1) output seed grid per the intersection rule above
    - For each of the (n_h+1) row separators and (n_v+1) col separators, pick a run length >= 1 ensuring at least one per axis is > 1 and total input dims are in [9,14]
    - Expand the seed by repeating each row/column by its run length to get the input
